{
"Buddhist logicians held that only particular things are real. There is no treeness shared by all trees.": "c333336c8d3c",
"Dharmakīrti developed the theory with the example of a tree. Every tree is different from every other. What they have in common is only that each is not a non-tree. The word tree draws a line around them by excluding everything else, and it is set up for those things that differ from non-trees. The mind that thinks tree sees many different things as one kind, and Dharmakīrti calls this cognition mistaken. A Tibetan commentary on his work, translated into Chinese by Fazun together with the verses, puts it plainly: the thought that takes separate trees as one thing is an inverted cognition. Yet it is not groundless. Dharmakīrti gives an analogy: Medicines differ, yet taken together or one at a time they are seen to cure fever and other illnesses, and other things do not. Different particulars can do the same work. That shared ability is the real basis of the concept, and there is no shared essence behind it.": "df0cbc544381",
"Every concept is a little mistaken, because it treats many things as one. Some of them still lead you to the jewel.": "2d09b9610124",
"Indian philosophers argued for centuries about universals. The Nyāya and Mīmāṃsā schools held that cowness is a real, eternal thing present in every cow, and that this is what the word cow names. The Buddhist logicians could not accept this. For them what is real is the particular, each thing in its own unique character at its own moment, and nothing real is shared by many things. That left them a problem. If there is no cowness, what does the word cow mean, and how can one word apply to many different animals? Dignāga answered in a chapter of his Compendium of the Means of Knowledge whose title the Chinese renders as Examination of Exclusion. The verse that opens it, in Lü Chengs Chinese translation from the Tibetan, reads: Knowledge arising from words is no different from inference. Like produced, it shows its own meaning by exclusion.": "f22568497186",
"Locke asked the Buddhist question in almost the same words. Since all things that exist are only particulars, how come we by general terms; or where find we those general natures they are supposed to stand for? His answer was that general and universal belong not to the real existence of things; but are the inventions and creatures of the understanding, made by it for its own use, and concern only signs. Like Dharmakīrti he did not make the sorting arbitrary. The kinds are the workmanship of the understanding, he wrote, but have their foundation in the similitude of things. The difference lies in how the general idea is formed. Locke thought the mind builds it positively, by leaving out the particular circumstances of time and place until a common core remains. Dharmakīrti thought there is no common core to keep and that the mind gets its general idea by excluding what the thing is not. Berkeley later attacked Lockes abstract ideas as impossible, and the Buddhist theory avoids that problem because it never posits them.": "0e8984ddb27b",
"Negation is a tool for clearing away wrong ideas. It should not become a way of sounding deep.": "d99374f99deb",
"On what exists, the closest is Locke. Both deny real universals, both make kinds a work of the mind, and both say the work is not arbitrary because it follows real likeness or real capacity in things. On how a word means, the closest is Saussure, since both define a word by what it excludes. On the Chinese sense of zhequan, the closest is Maimonides, who like Zongmi treats negation as the right way to approach what cannot be grasped, and also warns against negation for its own sake.": "a2269221be30",
"Saying salt is not bland removes an error. Saying it is salty tells you what it is.": "104d2b4d1ddf",
"Start with the old puzzle, which Westerners know from Plato and Locke. Point to two very different trees and ask what makes both of them trees. Most people will say they share something. Then give the Buddhist answer: they share nothing, except that neither is a non-tree, and both do what trees do. Mention Saussure for anyone who has studied linguistics, since the comparison will help them. Then tell the story of the two lights, because it carries the main point better than any argument. Every concept is a little mistaken, and the question is whether it gets you to the jewel. End with Zongmis salt, which is easy to remember. Not bland clears away an error, and salty is what you taste.": "75648577afa7",
"The Chinese term for the theory, zhequan, expression by negation, took on a second life in China. The ninth-century master Zongmi used it for a style of teaching. Negation removes what it is not. Affirmation shows what it is. When the sūtras say not arising, not ceasing, not defiled, not pure, they speak by negation, and he gave a homely example: To say salt is not bland is negation. To say it is salty is affirmation. He also gave a warning. Students of his day thought negation was profound and affirmation shallow, and so they valued only phrases like not mind, not Buddha. Zongmi said this came from loving words of negation and being unwilling to recognize the thing itself. The Indian theory and the Chinese teaching style are not the same idea, and this lesson treats both because they share a name and a lesson. Concepts are drawn by exclusion, and exclusion is not yet seeing.": "343e0ff4818b",
"The Swiss linguist Ferdinand de Saussure founded modern linguistics on the idea that a sign has no meaning on its own. Its value comes from its place in a system, from its differences from all the other signs. In the English translation of his lectures, A linguistic system is a series of differences of sound combined with a series of differences of ideas. The word sheep means what it means partly because English also has mutton, and a language with one word for both carves the world differently. Modern scholars have often compared Saussure with the apoha theory, and the likeness is real. Both say that a word is defined by what it excludes. The difference is in what each is explaining. Saussure was describing a social system of signs and set aside the question of how words connect to things. Dharmakīrti was explaining how concepts connect to real particulars, and his answer depends on the work those particulars do. Saussure stays inside language. Dharmakīrti is always asking whether a concept will get you to the jewel.": "b2b76f16f6a8",
"The first further step is the idea of a useful mistake. Western philosophy has usually divided concepts into true and false, or into those that match reality and those that do not. Dharmakīrti put them in a third place. Every concept misrepresents, because it shows many things as one. Some concepts still lead reliably to what we want, because they track real differences in what things can do, and others lead nowhere. This is the two truths again, applied to logic. Conventional thought is not true in the final sense, and it is not to be thrown away, because it is how anyone reasons toward the final sense at all. A teacher can say this plainly to a Western student: every word you use is a simplification, and some simplifications are maps that get you home.": "5702d294ae94",
"The likeness ends at the purpose. Locke wanted to map the limits of human understanding, and Saussure wanted a science of language. Both describe concepts and leave them in place. Dharmakīrti calls every concept mistaken, because it presents many things as one, and still useful, because it can lead to the jewel. He says the whole framework of conventional thought is set up by the wise in order to enter the ultimate. Concepts are a means. The practitioner uses them to reason, to teach, and to act, and comes in the end to a perception that does not need them. Neither Locke nor Saussure thought there was any such perception to come to.": "0a2a072fb5f9",
"The second step is Zongmis warning, which applies to every teacher who works in English. Western audiences drawn to Buddhism often love the language of negation: no self, emptiness, not this, not that. It can become a style, a way of sounding deep, and Zongmi saw the same thing among the Chan students of his day. They valued only not mind, not Buddha and would not recognize the thing itself. Maimonides gave the same warning in another tradition: negate by proof, not by mere words. The lesson for a teacher is to use negation the way Dharmakīrti and Maimonides used it, as a disciplined way to remove a specific wrong idea, one at a time. It should never be a mood. After the negations there is still something to see, and the point of all the clearing is to see it.": "f5a40cb512cf",
"The word tree works by excluding everything that is not a tree.": "ce05dd90e822",
"What grounds the word is that different trees do the same work, as different medicines all cure a fever.": "79cbd871f496",
"Zongmis sense of the word has its own Western parallel in negative theology, and its sharpest statement is by the Jewish philosopher Maimonides. In the Guide for the Perplexed he wrote, Know that the negative attributes of God are the true attributes. His illustration is a ship. One person knows only the word. Others learn, one step at a time, that it is not an accident, not a mineral, not a plant growing in the earth, not a flat object like boards or doors, not a sphere, and so on, until the tenth has almost arrived at the correct notion. In the same way one comes nearer to knowing God by learning what God is not. Then comes a warning very like Zongmis: You must be careful, in what you negative, to negative by proof, not by mere words. Negation, for both, is a discipline and should not be treated as a mood or a style. The difference is the goal. Maimonides held that Gods essence stays beyond human understanding however far the negations go. Zongmi held that after the negations one must recognize the thing itself, the knowing that is the nature of mind.": "1833134f46d8"
}
